 {
  "source": "Deutsche Bank",
  "title": "The end of the safety net: Life without the macroeconomic stabilisers",
  "type": "macro",
  "priority": "medium",
  "published": "2026-09-28",
  "insights": [
   "Financial markets and the economy have historically relied on an implicit safety net consisting of a monetary put from central banks and a fiscal put from governments to smooth the business cycle.",
   "These policy stabilisers, which reached their peak during the Covid-19 pandemic, are now constrained by high sovereign yields, record debt-to-GDP ratios, and persistent supply-driven inflation.",
   "The current environment echoes the 1970s stagflationary period and the pre-WWI gold standard era, where policy tools were either blunted or limited.",
   "Absent a sharp decline in yields or a collapse in inflation, the report concludes that higher macroeconomic volatility is likely to persist in the coming years."
  ],
  "macro": [
   {
    "indicator": "Sovereign yields",
    "reading": "multi-year highs",
    "interpretation": "Constrains fiscal policy and limits the government's ability to act as a macroeconomic stabiliser.",
    "status": "level",
    "period": "current"
   },
   {
    "indicator": "Debt-to-GDP ratios",
    "reading": "highest in several decades",
    "interpretation": "Limits fiscal space for future government intervention during downturns.",
    "status": "level",
    "period": "current"
   }
  ],
  "risks": [
   "Macroeconomic fluctuations, interest rate increases, inflation surprises, fiscal funding needs, and FX depreciation."
  ],
  "charts": [
   "Figure 1 plots the length of US economic expansions in months, showing that the last five expansions all rank in the seven longest."
  ],
  "vol_positioning": [
   "The report argues that the removal of traditional monetary and fiscal safety nets will lead to structurally higher macroeconomic volatility."
  ],
  "entities": [
   {
    "name": "NBER",
    "ticker": "",
    "class": "other"
   }
  ],
  "theme_stances": [
   {
    "theme": "macroeconomic policy regime shift",
    "stance": "supportive",
    "conviction": "medium",
    "key_argument": "The era of aggressive monetary and fiscal intervention is ending due to structural constraints like high debt and supply-side inflation.",
    "primary_instruments": [],
    "vs_consensus": "",
    "evidence": "these old stabilisers now face major obstacles"
   }
  ]
 },
